1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,297
Halliday G2. This story started with unboxing a new pair of smart glasses.

2
00:00:08,297 --> 00:00:10,690
It ended with our own app running inside them.

3
00:00:10,870 --> 00:00:13,903
Between those two points came frustration with limited features,

4
00:00:13,903 --> 00:00:16,272
firmware analysis, and even a homemade data cable.

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,371
The first impression is tidy: a plain shipping box,

6
00:00:19,371 --> 00:00:22,660
separate product packaging inside, and then the glasses case.

7
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,824
The kit includes instructions, small accessories, and a magnetic charging cable.

8
00:00:26,824 --> 00:00:28,954
The unboxing feels like a finished product.

9
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:32,355
At first glance, the black frames look like ordinary glasses.

10
00:00:32,355 --> 00:00:35,849
Thicker arms, controls, and the optics hint at the electronics inside.

11
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:38,377
The manufacturer offers notifications, calls,

12
00:00:38,377 --> 00:00:42,060
music, translation, notes, and a meeting assistant. There is no camera.

13
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:44,954
That alone does not mean all data is processed locally.

14
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:48,011
The previous generation used ordinary optical lenses.

15
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:50,814
A separate projection module directed the image toward

16
00:00:50,814 --> 00:00:53,514
your eye, in the upper corner of your field of view.

17
00:00:53,514 --> 00:00:56,576
To us, those glasses looked more discreet from the outside.

18
00:00:56,710 --> 00:00:59,427
The new generation uses waveguides inside the lenses.

19
00:00:59,427 --> 00:01:03,118
Visible optical projection areas give away that these are smart glasses.

20
00:01:03,118 --> 00:01:05,424
We can see that difference in our own photos.

21
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,309
But the new image looks clearer to us, the projection area

22
00:01:08,309 --> 00:01:11,119
feels larger, and viewing is more comfortable than before.

23
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:13,686
This is our experience, not a laboratory measurement.

24
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,560
We wanted more: our own navigation, speech recognition models of our

25
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:20,873
choice, voice commands, and the freedom to build apps ourselves.

26
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:22,956
This is where the disappointment began.

27
00:01:22,956 --> 00:01:25,727
In the manufacturer documentation we checked, we found no

28
00:01:25,727 --> 00:01:28,742
public development kit or official way to install our own app.

29
00:01:28,742 --> 00:01:31,610
We were left with the features the manufacturer had chosen.

30
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:35,836
Official updates do exist. But updating the glasses and independently extending

31
00:01:35,836 --> 00:01:38,028
their capabilities are two different things.

32
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,373
So we decided to investigate our own device.

33
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:42,646
First, we needed to understand the firmware it

34
00:01:42,646 --> 00:01:45,017
receives and what that firmware actually allows.

35
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,129
We built a desktop tool that signs in with the owner account,

36
00:01:48,129 --> 00:01:51,534
downloads the update offered for the glasses, and saves it on the computer.

37
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:54,940
That let us analyze system code, commands, and startup scripts.

38
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:57,836
The downloaded update differed from the version installed

39
00:01:57,836 --> 00:02:00,426
on the glasses, so we kept their analysis separate.

40
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:03,883
The analysis led to commands authenticated with the owner account.

41
00:02:03,883 --> 00:02:06,415
These could enable Wi-Fi and a file transfer service.

42
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,178
FTP became our route into the glasses file system.

43
00:02:09,178 --> 00:02:13,612
Through our own phone app, we could read files and save parts of the installed system.

44
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:16,604
Before making changes, we saved the entire 16 MiB

45
00:02:16,604 --> 00:02:19,379
main flash memory and important configuration areas.

46
00:02:19,379 --> 00:02:21,673
We compared repeated reads with each other.

47
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:23,770
But a backup does not guarantee recovery.

48
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:26,673
If the system stopped booting, we would also need a connection

49
00:02:26,673 --> 00:02:29,529
and a way to write the backup without relying on that system.

50
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:33,393
Next, the four contacts on the magnetic connector caught our attention.

51
00:02:33,393 --> 00:02:35,017
Our supplied cable carried power.

52
00:02:35,017 --> 00:02:36,493
For data, we had to modify it.

53
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:38,125
Wires alone were not enough.

54
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:41,484
In the installed firmware, we found a disabled USB debugging flag.

55
00:02:41,484 --> 00:02:45,047
Making a data cable and enabling that setting were two separate tasks.

56
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:48,835
After making backups, changing the specific setting, and restarting,

57
00:02:48,835 --> 00:02:52,360
the computer finally recognized the glasses as a composite USB device.

58
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:54,280
We also gained command-line access.

59
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,580
That let us inspect the running system and test our own executable

60
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:00,580
modules without first replacing the entire operating system.

61
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:02,665
The first clear win was MINDYLAB.

62
00:03:02,665 --> 00:03:05,226
This was our own program, with a separate screen,

63
00:03:05,226 --> 00:03:08,571
custom drawing, and a counter responding to taps on the glasses.

64
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:12,336
Sending text to the built-in notes card had only been an intermediate step.

65
00:03:12,336 --> 00:03:15,040
Now our code was running inside the glasses themselves.

66
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:18,342
Later, we saved the module on the glasses and added automatic

67
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:21,391
loading through a small startup patch to the original firmware.

68
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,122
After a restart, the phone app could start and stop our

69
00:03:24,122 --> 00:03:26,601
screen without connecting the glasses to a computer.

70
00:03:26,601 --> 00:03:29,604
Taps work, and stopping the app brings back the normal display.

71
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:32,270
This proves that an additional app is possible.

72
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:34,755
For now, though, we have a prototype for a specific

73
00:03:34,755 --> 00:03:36,898
firmware version, not a universal app store.

74
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,482
Navigation, local meeting transcription, and our choice of models remain future stages.

75
00:03:41,482 --> 00:03:43,524
The current companion app still uses the

76
00:03:43,524 --> 00:03:46,179
manufacturer account to authenticate the connection.

77
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,345
We have already made a small patch to the original system.

78
00:03:49,345 --> 00:03:51,749
But a complete custom firmware build and reliable

79
00:03:51,749 --> 00:03:54,644
restoration after a failed boot have not yet been achieved.

80
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:59,298
We are still investigating independent recovery, the flash writing path, and boot checks.

81
00:03:59,298 --> 00:04:02,569
Those are needed to give bigger changes a more reliable way back.

82
00:04:02,730 --> 00:04:07,077
Our verdict: the hardware has promise, but the freedom to customize it was
disappointing.

83
00:04:07,077 --> 00:04:09,519
A closed platform pushed us to find our own route.

84
00:04:09,710 --> 00:04:13,020
That route has already led to an extra app inside the glasses.

85
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:16,918
Next, we want more privacy, local processing, and real freedom to create.

86
00:04:16,918 --> 00:04:20,388
These are goals, not a claim that everything is already finished.

87
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,050
Our research findings, tools, app source code,

88
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:26,313
and documentation are available in the project GitHub repository.

89
00:04:26,313 --> 00:04:29,125
The full link is on screen and in the video description.

90
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:31,942
These tools are experimental and provided as is.

91
00:04:31,942 --> 00:04:35,795
You accept full responsibility and use them entirely at your own risk.

92
00:04:35,795 --> 00:04:40,473
We provide no guarantees of operation, safety, compatibility, or successful recovery.

93
00:04:40,473 --> 00:04:42,730
Device damage and data loss are possible.
